From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm cuda: the EXL3 prompt experts take a whole 2,048-row chunk in one call

Our fork `ai-engine`, ref `fork-ssh/ovn-up`, commit 0aec07b2f "glm cuda: the
EXL3 prompt experts take a whole 2,048-row chunk in one call". The fork's hunk
applies to this tree unchanged; only its doc comment is reflowed, because the
fork writes two lines and this repo's lean gate takes a comment on one. The
figures the fork's second line carries, 57 rows an expert against 28, are in
the paragraph below instead.

cuda_exl3.chunk_rows was 1,024 while a prompt chunk is prefill_rows = 2,048, so
run()'s loop called the routed experts twice a chunk. Each call reads every
expert's trellis in 64-row tiles, so with 288 experts and top-8 routing a call
of 1,024 rows gives an expert about 28 rows, its tile 44 per cent full, and
every expert is read twice a chunk. One call of 2,048 rows gives about 57 rows,
89 per cent, and reads each expert once.

It completes none of our patches: zig/src/families/glm/cuda_exl3.zig is a pin
file that no patch 0001 to 0042 touches, and the pin has the same 1,024.

Every use of chunk_rows, and what it costs:

- run() at the loop: `while (at < rows) : (at += chunk_rows)`, one pass a chunk
  instead of two, with `n = @min(chunk_rows, rows - at)` now the chunk's rows.
- picksBytes(rows) and scratchBytes(rows, ...) and run()'s `cap`, all through
  `@min(rows, chunk_rows)`: the arena the engine allocates for a prompt buffer
  set grows from a 1,024-row call's to a 2,048-row call's.
- the existing test "a chunk's compacted picks end before the common scratch at
  every row count" walks 1 .. 3 * chunk_rows, so it now walks twice as far; it
  still passes, it is just a longer loop.

The extra bytes a rank, from exl3_prompt.sizes and picksBytes at dims 4096 and
a 2,048-row prompt buffer (GLM's prompt path is the fused one: prompt.eligible
holds at mcg, uniform_k2 8, gu_stride 0, dims % 256 and width % 128):

  rank share    gate and up share a rotation    they do not
  640 wide      +153,124,864 B (146.0 MiB)      +278,953,984 B (266.0 MiB)
  768 wide      +155,222,016 B (148.0 MiB)      +281,051,136 B (268.0 MiB)

640 and 768 are TP=3's narrow and wide expert shares of 2,048. 32,768 B of each
figure is the compacted picks; the rest is the rotated inputs, the act and y at
2,048 rows. weights.exl3_shx defaults true and cuda_weights.zig clears it only
when a layer's experts do not share one input rotation, so the left column is
the one to expect on our checkpoint; the fork's own "~270 MiB a rank" is the
right column. A twin prompt buffer set adds nothing: Buffers.make zeroes every
size a twin does not hold, and the four EXL3 sizes are not in twin_at. A decode
window is unaffected, its rows are far below chunk_rows either way.

The bits do not change, and that is checkable here rather than taken from the
commit message: the prompt kernels tf_exl3_prompt_gu and tf_exl3_prompt_down
take K whole, with no split-k argument and no k axis in their grids (the grid
is width or dims over 128 or 256, by item), so one pair accumulates over the
same K in the same order whatever else is in the call. Grouping only decides
which 64-pair item a pair lands in, and a pair's arithmetic does not depend on
its neighbours.

Added here, not in the fork's commit: a test that the arena the engine asks for
holds a whole chunk, over TP=3's two shares, TP=2 and TP=1 and both rotation
layouts, and then pins the four byte totals above exactly. It is the invariant
the change exists for, and it fails on the pin's 1,024 because scratchBytes
then sizes the arena for half a chunk. The exact totals are there so the cost a
rank is a number the gate holds rather than one in a report. It reads
prefill_rows from cuda_state.zig, the same import the fork's own later form of
this constant uses.

One note for whoever integrates the pending item-plan patch (work/p41/
0042-exl3-decode-items.patch): measured with `git apply --check`, that patch
takes the stack without this lane cleanly, and on top of it its last
cuda_exl3.zig hunk is refused, because that hunk appends its items test after
the file's last test and this patch added a test there. It is an append past
an append, nothing more.

What a GPU run has to show: that a prompt reply digest is unchanged, and that
the pool still fits. The second is the real risk, not the first: this takes
about 150 MiB a rank of headroom on a TP=3 Spark, so a prompt whose buffers
only just fitted before may now fail to allocate. Worth a boot at the served
context before the digest run.

Re-seated on v1.2.7 (93a2e93ecebff8aa52b88e58c5dd222516e69c5f, from pin 9c9bf542) by the 2026-11-12 re-seat (work/reseat/DECISIONS.md):
- cuda_exl3.zig has no commit in 9c9bf542..v1.2.7 (206 lines both); applies as is, test included.
- source: work/fold/0032-exl3-chunk-rows.patch

diff --git a/zig/src/families/glm/cuda_exl3.zig b/zig/src/families/glm/cuda_exl3.zig
index 61b5ebdbe5dc1770fc3b5352faa70648539394e4..3dd0ab083490ff5ed029c820246b6b0bd1379e52 100644
--- a/zig/src/families/glm/cuda_exl3.zig
+++ b/zig/src/families/glm/cuda_exl3.zig
@@ -126,8 +126,8 @@ pub const Kernels = cuda.exl3_experts.Experts;
 const common = cuda.exl3_experts;
 pub const Scratch = struct { picks: u64, xg: u64, xu: u64, z: u64 };
 pub const top_k = 8;
-/// Rows a call to the common experts takes: each call reads every expert's weights, so a prompt pass needs few calls.
-pub const chunk_rows = 1024;
+/// Rows a call to the common experts takes: each call reads every expert's weights, so a prompt chunk is one call.
+pub const chunk_rows = 2048;
 fn layer(w: Experts, count: usize, dims: usize, width: usize, limit: f32) common.Layer {
     return .{ .gate = w.gt, .up = w.ut, .down = w.dt, .gate_k2 = w.k2, .up_k2 = w.k2, .down_k2 = w.k2, .suh_g = w.suh_g, .suh_u = w.suh_u, .svh_g = w.svh_g, .svh_u = w.svh_u, .suh_d = w.suh_d, .svh_d = w.svh_d, .experts = count, .dims = dims, .width = width, .codebook = .mcg, .limit = limit, .act_mode = 0, .uniform_k2 = 8, .shared_input = w.shx };
 }
@@ -228,3 +228,19 @@ test "a decode window's item plan is at GLM's shapes the plain layout's bytes, s
         try std.testing.expectEqual(picksBytes(rows) + plain, scratchBytes(rows, 4096, width, false, false)[0]);
     };
 }
+
+test "a prompt chunk's routed experts are one call: its arena holds a whole chunk, not half of one" {
+    const prefill_rows = @import("cuda_state.zig").prefill_rows;
+    try std.testing.expect(chunk_rows >= prefill_rows); // run()'s loop takes a whole chunk in one pass
+    for ([_]usize{ 640, 768, 1024, 2048 }) |width| for ([_]bool{ false, true }) |shx| { // TP=3's two shares, TP=2, TP=1
+        var l = shape(4096, width);
+        l.shared_input = shx;
+        const whole = try common.prompt.bytes(l, prefill_rows, top_k);
+        try std.testing.expectEqual(picksBytes(prefill_rows) + whole, scratchBytes(prefill_rows, 4096, width, true, shx)[0]);
+    };
+    // the arena at TP=3's two shares, so what this costs a rank is a number the gate holds, not one in a report
+    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(usize, 306_249_728), scratchBytes(prefill_rows, 4096, 640, true, true)[0]);
+    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(usize, 310_444_032), scratchBytes(prefill_rows, 4096, 768, true, true)[0]);
+    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(usize, 557_907_968), scratchBytes(prefill_rows, 4096, 640, true, false)[0]);
+    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(usize, 562_102_272), scratchBytes(prefill_rows, 4096, 768, true, false)[0]);
+}
